export const DE_C1_S06_ENGLISH: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "de-dr-spitzbueb": [
    `In Lea Gysin's childhood there were three days a year when her father wasn't there, and they were precisely the three days when all of Basel went mad. When the lights in the city centre went out at four in the morning for the Morgestraich and the drums started up, Peter Gysin, director of a small, very discreet private bank, was supposedly long since sitting in a hotel in the Engadin. "Carnival," he said, "is a racket you take three days off for, only to regret it for a year afterwards." Lea went with her friends and, to be honest, didn't mind that he was missing.`,
    `She especially loved the Schnitzelbänke: groups or single performers who went from pub to pub with masks over their faces, held up a painted picture and sang malicious verses about the events of the year. The most famous of all performed alone and called himself "Dr Spitzbueb". Nobody knew who was behind his mask, a long-nosed, grinning figure in a top hat. His verses about bankers were so precise, so mean and so spot-on that people in the city whispered he must once have worked on the Paradeplatz in Zurich himself, which for a Basler was the worst insinuation imaginable.`,
    `In February, two weeks before carnival, her father fell on the icy steps in front of his house and broke his hip. Lea, by now over forty herself, drove over to fetch a few things for the hospital: pyjamas, reading glasses, the Zurich newspaper he read only to get annoyed by it. In the cupboard in the attic, behind the winter tyres, she found a hatbox. Inside were a black top hat, a mask with a long nose and a rolled-up picture of a banker in swimming trunks floating down the Rhine on a sack of money.`,
    `It took her a while to understand. Then she sat down on the floor among old tax files and laughed until she cried. The Engadin. Thirty years of the Engadin! In the box there were also notes in his meticulous handwriting, the verses for this year, neatly numbered, with corrections in red, as if they were a quarterly report.`,
    `In hospital her father lay pale and irritable in bed and complained about the food. Lea put the hatbox on the blanket. He looked at it for a long time, then looked out of the window, towards the Rhine. "Your mother knew," he said at last. "From the very first year. She chose the mask for me." For forty years, he said, he had had to manage other people's money during the day and smile pleasantly while he did it. "It had to go somewhere."`,
    `Then he did something he had never done before: he asked her for a favour. Dr Spitzbueb had not missed a single carnival in thirty years, he said, and he had no intention of starting because of a hip. Lea protested. She couldn't sing, she didn't know the pubs, she was a primary school teacher, for heaven's sake. "Exactly," said her father. "You can stand in front of people who don't want to listen to you. That's all there is to it."`,
    `On Monday evening she stood in top hat and mask in a packed pub on the Spalenberg, the picture high above her head, and sang verse after verse in a shaky voice. The place went wild. The last verse her father had written only in hospital, in pencil, on the back of the menu. Translated from Basel German, it goes like this: "A bank director, stern and grey, / goes to the Engadin each year, they say. / Three days of quiet, snow and wine - / or so he tells them back at home."`,
    `People laughed without quite knowing what at. Only one person really laughed: the man at the other end of the line, who had been listening to everything through the phone in Lea's costume pocket. When she took off the mask outside in the cold alley, he was still there. "Too slow in the second verse," he said. "But otherwise not bad. Next year I'm going to the Engadin again."`,
  ],
  "de-die-elster-und-die-wahrheit": [
    `In the floodplain forest on the Elbe, not far from Wittenberge, there lived a magpie called Agathe who collected everything that glittered: bottle caps, tinfoil, once even a wedding ring whose loss had landed an angler from Perleberg in a serious marital crisis. But one spring Agathe made a discovery that changed her life. Nothing, she realised, glittered as much as a piece of news.`,
    `It started harmlessly. She flew from tree to tree and reported what she had seen: that the beaver was building a new dam, that the wild boar had had young. The animals listened politely and went their way again. Then, one rainy Tuesday, Agathe saw the fox with a dead hare in its mouth. "The fox has eaten a hare," she announced. That was true, but not new; foxes had always done that. So she improved the report a little: "The fox is planning to eat all the hares. All of them!" Never before had anyone listened to her so spellbound.`,
    `From then on there was no stopping her. Agathe founded, if you want to call it that, the "Floodplain Forest Messenger", which came out three times a day, namely whenever she was hungry for attention. The beaver, it said, was secretly damming the water to drown the mice. The storks had not come back from the south at all, but had never been away. Whether any of it was true soon interested nobody. It glittered, and that was enough.`,
    `Only the old eagle owl Hildegard, who had lived in a hollow poplar for thirty years, shook her head, as far as an eagle owl can. She knew more about the forest than everyone else put together, but her knowledge had one decisive drawback: it took time, and it was mostly boring. "The beavers dam the water because they are beavers," she said when anyone asked her. People asked her less and less.`,
    `The one who grasped the new situation fastest was, of course, the fox. He went to see Agathe, praised her courage, her independence and her beautiful feathers, and from then on supplied her with tips "from well-informed circles". That was how the forest learned that Hildegard was hoarding stocks of mice, that she spied into the songbirds' nests at night and wasn't from round here at all. Soon nobody greeted the old owl any more. The blackbirds hissed when she flew past.`,
    `In autumn the fox delivered his most important report. The owl, he whispered, was planning a major attack on the hedges on the night before Sunday. For safety, all the hares should gather at midnight on the big clearing, where they could be better protected. Agathe spread it before breakfast. It was her biggest story; it glittered like nothing before.`,
    `At midnight twenty-three hares sat trembling in the moonlight on the clearing. Agathe perched on a branch above them, because she wanted to be there when her story came true. Then she saw something red moving in the bushes at the edge of the clearing, and something else, and something else again: the fox had brought his brothers. And for the first time in months Agathe understood a story before she told it.`,
    `She screamed as loudly as only a magpie can scream: "Foxes in the bushes! Three of them! Run!" It was the truth, and luckily it was at least as exciting as a lie. The hares scattered in all directions, and the foxes went home hungry. Since then Agathe has never made anything up again. She only reports what she sees: beaver dams, baby boars, the weather. Hardly anyone listens to her any more. But the eagle owl Hildegard, whom people greet again by now, says that is the surest proof that Agathe is now telling the truth.`,
  ],
  "de-der-marillenbaum": [
    `Vienna, 3 July. Dear Ms Novak, As you are the new tenant of plot 15, allow me to draw your attention to a circumstance that may have escaped your notice. The branches of your apricot tree now reach a good metre and a half over the fence into my garden. Since mid-June I have been collecting fallen fruit from my lawn every day, fruit that moreover attracts wasps in such numbers as to spoil my time outdoors. I therefore politely request that you cut the tree back immediately, by 15 July at the latest. With my highest respects, Hofrat Dr Ferdinand Pichler, plot 14.`,
    `Vienna, 5 July. Dear Herr Hofrat, Thank you very much for your letter. I've never received such a beautiful one, certainly not about fruit. Unfortunately I have to disappoint you: I'm not cutting a tree in the middle of the harvest, it would break my heart. But you're very welcome to keep the apricots that land on your side; this year they're especially sweet. Best wishes from number 15, Dragana Novak. P.S. Basil helps against wasps, my mother says.`,
    `Vienna, 8 July. Dear Ms Novak, I do not eat apricots. I would further draw your attention to section 422 of the General Civil Code, according to which every owner may himself remove the branches of another's tree hanging over his land, namely at his own expense and with the greatest possible care for the tree. I intend to make use of this right on Saturday, 20 July, at 9 o'clock. Yours faithfully, Dr F. Pichler.`,
    `Vienna, 10 July. Dear Herr Hofrat, That is of course your right, and I won't stand in your way. I'll bring coffee on Saturday. By the way, the chairman of our club told me that the tree was planted in 1979, by a Frau Gertrude Pichler. Is that a coincidence? You don't have to answer. Yours, Dragana Novak.`,
    `Vienna, 21 July. Dear Ms Novak, You will have noticed that I did not appear on Saturday. I owe you an explanation. Plots 14 and 15 were once both ours. My wife planted the apricot in the first year of our marriage, as a joke, because I claimed nothing decent would ever grow in Floridsdorf. After her death thirteen years ago I did not renew the lease for number 15; two gardens were too much for one person. Since then I have not eaten a single apricot. Every one that falls on my lawn is a memory I did not order. I ask you to excuse the tone of my previous letters. F. Pichler.`,
    `Vienna, 22 July. Dear Herr Pichler, There's a jar of jam on your fence post. On Sunday at three I'm baking an apricot cake, from Gerti's tree, and I have far too much dough. You don't have to eat anything. You're allowed to just watch. I'll prune the tree in the autumn, if you show me how she would have pruned it. Yours, Dragana.`,
    `Vienna, 29 July. Dear, kind Ms Novak, Thank you very much for Sunday afternoon. I owed you the whipped cream that I had forgotten, and am therefore enclosing a little packet of coffee. As regards the pruning, after careful consideration I have come to the conclusion that the branch over my compost heap may stay. The others likewise. With warm regards, yours, Ferdinand Pichler. P.S. The cake was, I must confess, better than Gerti's. I ask you not to tell anyone in the club.`,
  ],
  "de-der-leere-raum": [
    `After thirty-eight years at the Volkswagen plant in Baunatal, Jürgen Hellwig had managed exactly four weeks of retirement before his wife suggested he find something to do, preferably outside the house. That was how, in June, he came to be wearing a dark blue waistcoat marked "Attendant" and guarding a room at the documenta in Kassel for a hundred days. The room was on the first floor of the Fridericianum, painted white, about six metres by eight and completely empty. Hellwig had never been to an exhibition, unless you counted the annual show of the local rabbit breeders' club. Next to the door hung a small sign: "M. Ostrowski, Untitled".`,
    `What on earth was he supposed to be guarding here? There was nothing to steal, nothing to touch, nothing you had to keep three steps away from. In the first few days he felt like a porter in front of a demolished house. Visitors photographed the walls, some laughed, a gentleman from Düsseldorf demanded to speak to the curator, and a school class from Göttingen loudly tested whether there was an echo. There was. Hellwig told the pupils off and felt rather ridiculous doing it.`,
    `Then something strange happened. Because there was nothing else in the room, people looked at him. They asked him what the work meant, and at first he made up answers he had picked up in other rooms: emptiness as resistance, absence as presence. Over time his answers grew shorter and more honest. "No idea," he said, "but in here you can hear very well when it's raining outside." People would often stay for quite a while then, and some told him things you don't normally tell a stranger in a waistcoat.`,
    `One visitor came every day. She was perhaps in her mid-thirties, sat in the corner with a notebook and looked not at the walls but at him. Hellwig took her at first for an inspector from the security firm, then for a critic, and finally for someone with too much time on her hands. In the third week she asked him what he used to do. He told her about the hall, the assembly line, the gearboxes he had assembled for thirty-eight years, four hundred a shift. "And did anyone ever look at you while you did it?" she asked. He thought about it. "The foreman. When something went wrong."`,
    `On the hundredth day the curator appeared with a photographer, and the woman from the corner was with her. She introduced herself: Marta Ostrowski. Then she handed him the catalogue, which he had not opened once in a hundred days, and showed him the page. There, in two sentences, it said: "The room is not empty. The work consists of the person who guards it."`,
    `Hellwig read it three times. A hundred days. A work of art. Him, Jürgen Hellwig from Baunatal, who hadn't even been in the front row of his own wedding album. He didn't know whether to feel flattered or tricked, and decided on both. The artist asked whether he would allow the museum in Frankfurt that had bought the work to give his name. Another attendant would be standing there in future. Hellwig nodded. "But tell your colleague," he said, "that in here you can hear very well when it's raining outside."`,
    `That evening he told his wife everything. She listened without interrupting him, which was rare, and then only asked: "And? What did they pay for you?" He didn't know. He hadn't asked. For the first time in thirty-eight years it hadn't mattered to him.`,
  ],
  "de-die-pilzstelle": [
    `Gottfried Haslbeck, retired presiding judge at the regional court, had heard many secrets in his life and kept most of them to himself. But the most important was his own: a spot on the slope below the Falkenstein, between a fallen spruce and a moss-covered boulder, where in September the porcini mushrooms stood like spectators in a grandstand. His father had shown it to him in 1962, on condition that he never give it away. Not even his wife Rosi knew about it. He had kept it from her for forty-two years, which he considered a considerable achievement.`,
    `One Tuesday in September he found the spot plundered. Not laid waste, which would almost have been more comforting, but carefully harvested: cleanly cut stems, no trampled moss, the very small mushrooms left standing so they could grow back. No day-tripper had been at work here. This had been an expert. Haslbeck stood in front of the fallen spruce for a long time and felt something he hadn't felt since retiring: the ambition to solve a case.`,
    `He went about it the way he was used to. At the kitchen table he opened a file, numbered the pieces of evidence and drew up a list of suspects. There was the neighbour, Sepp Kronawitter, who claimed every autumn that he found "nothing, nothing at all", and still smelled of mushrooms. There was the young forester, who wandered about the woods too much to be innocent. And there were the holidaymakers from the north, who roamed the Bavarian Forest in outdoor jackets and photographed everything that didn't run away. Rosi looked over his shoulder for a while, told him not to forget his snack, and went out into the garden.`,
    `The questioning of Sepp Kronawitter at the garden fence produced nothing; the man was lying, but obviously about something else. So Haslbeck turned to modern investigative methods. At the DIY store in Regen he bought a wildlife camera, the kind hunters strap to trees to photograph deer and wild boar at night, and fixed it to the spruce with trembling fingers. Three days later he collected the memory card and sat down at the kitchen table with his granddaughter's laptop.`,
    `The pictures showed a fox, two roe deer and, on Thursday at 5:43 a.m., a figure in a green loden jacket bending over the porcini with a basket. He knew the jacket. It was his own, which he hadn't worn for years and which hung in the hall on the third hook. He knew the basket too. And he knew the way the figure steadied itself with one hand on the boulder, because of the knee she had twisted skiing twenty years ago.`,
    `Rosi confessed without beating about the bush, before he could even present the evidence. She had known the spot since 1983, she said. She had followed him back then because she thought he was meeting another woman, and then she had been very relieved, and a little disappointed, that it was only mushrooms. Since then she had gone there every year a few days before him and always left him enough to be pleased. Only this year the landlord of the "Hirschen" in Zwiesel had needed more than usual. He paid well, after all. And the cruise he had considered nonsense for forty years had to be paid for somehow.`,
    `Haslbeck was silent for a long time. Then he thought of the "porcini in cream with bread dumplings" at the "Hirschen", which he recommended to every visitor from the city, remarking that you only found mushrooms this good if you knew where to look. He announced his verdict over dinner: acquittal, on one condition. Next year they would go together. Rosi nodded. At half past five, she said. And he should wear the loden jacket himself; it suited him better anyway.`,
  ],
};

